JOB 18 — Lambda & Recursion
Topic: Lambda Function and Recursion
1. Aim
To understand lambda functions and recursive functions in Python.
2. Lambda Function
A lambda function is a small anonymous function written in a single line.
Syntax
lambda arguments: expression
Example
check = lambda p: p[1] > 25.05

part = ("PE-0137", 25.09)

print(check(part))
Output
True
Lambda functions are useful when a small function is needed only once.
Sorting using lambda
parts = [
    ("PE-0137", 25.02),
    ("PE-0201", 24.90),
    ("PE-0345", 25.09)
]

sorted_parts = sorted(parts, key=lambda p: p[1])

print(sorted_parts)
Output
[('PE-0201', 24.9), ('PE-0137', 25.02), ('PE-0345', 25.09)]
3. Recursion
Recursion is a process in which a function calls itself to solve a problem.
A recursive function must have:
Base case — stops recursion.
Recursive step — calls the function again with a smaller/simpler problem.
Example
def count_parts(item):
    if item["children"] == []:
        return item["qty"]

    total = 0
    for child in item["children"]:
        total += count_parts(child)

    return total + item["qty"]
Here, count_parts() calls itself for each child part.
Key Points
Lambda = small one-line anonymous function.
Recursion = function calling itself.
Every recursive function needs a base case.
Without a base case, Python may produce RecursionError.
